---
// Standalone FAQ — the destination the old site's /faqs URL pointed at, and
// the page that owns the site-wide question set. The homepage keeps a short
// version and links here; program pages keep their own per-program sets.
//
// Questions are grouped, because a flat list of fifteen is a wall: buying,
// delivery, trust, payment. The shared <Faq> component supplies the core
// answers (which stay wired to site.json's verified flags); the page adds the
// site-wide questions that only make sense off a program page.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { site, live, quoteFormReady } from "../lib/site";

const airlines = (await getCollection("airlines"))
  .map((e) => ({ slug: e.id, ...e.data }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.airline.localeCompare(b.airline));
const verified = airlines.filter((a) => a.priceVerified);
const cheapest = [...verified].sort((a, b) => a.pricePerMile - b.pricePerMile)[0];
const fastest = [...airlines]
  .filter((a) => a.deliveryVerified)
  .sort((a, b) => parseInt(a.delivery.replace("Within ", ""), 10) - parseInt(b.delivery.replace("Within ", ""), 10))[0];

// The rate range is derived, so this answer can never drift from the data.
const rates = verified.map((a) => a.pricePerMile).sort((a, b) => a - b);
const rateRange = rates.length ? `${rates[0]}¢ to ${rates[rates.length - 1]}¢` : "";

// Site-wide questions that don't belong on a single program's page. Every
// answer here is true by construction or drawn from confirmed data — nothing
// asserts a client fact that isn't verified.
//
// Several are carried over from the old WordPress FAQ, which published all
// eight of its questions on the home page AND on /faqs, in identical wording
// and with no FAQPage markup on either. Same questions, two URLs, competing:
// they live here only, and this page is the one that emits the markup.
const extras = [
  {
    q: "Which airline programs can I buy miles for?",
    a: `We cover ${airlines.length} programs today, from ${airlines[0].airline} to ${airlines[airlines.length - 1].airline}, across Star Alliance, Oneworld, SkyTeam and the independent carriers. Each has its own page with its rate, order limits and delivery window. If yours isn't listed, send a request — not listed doesn't mean not available.`,
  },
  {
    q: "How do I order miles?",
    a: "Price the amount on any program page, then send the request with your name and email. We reply with a firm quote confirming the exact rate and delivery window. Nothing is charged on this website, and we only ask for your loyalty account number once you've approved that quote.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need to open a new account?",
    a: "No. The miles are transferred into the loyalty account you already hold, and you book the award yourself, directly with the airline. The reservation is in your name and stays under your control. We are not affiliated with any airline or loyalty program.",
  },
  {
    q: "Are taxes and carrier surcharges included?",
    a: "No. You're buying miles; the airline bills award taxes and any carrier surcharges separately when you book the seat. Those vary by route and program, and some programs pass on far less than others, which is worth checking before you decide how many miles to buy.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if the program I want is out of stock?",
    a: "Availability moves with sourcing, and a program showing limited availability today may open up shortly. Send the request anyway: we'll tell you honestly whether we can fill it and when, rather than taking an order we can't deliver.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I buy miles for someone else?",
    a: "Tell us on the request and we'll confirm what's possible for that specific program. Rules on transferring into another person's account differ from program to program, so it's a question we answer per case rather than with a blanket yes.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use miles and cash together to book a flight?",
    a: "Some programs let you pay part of an award in miles and settle the rest in cash. Others don't, and want the full mileage price or nothing. Where a program has no cash-and-points option, topping the account up to the full amount is the way through, which is the situation most people are in when they find us.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many miles does it take to buy a plane ticket?",
    a: "It depends on the route and the cabin far more than on the fare: a short domestic economy seat can go for a fraction of what a long-haul business class one costs, and the same seat is priced differently by each program. The airline's own award search, or its pay-with-miles option, gives you the exact number for your dates. Our guide to how many miles a flight costs works through the typical amounts by region and cabin.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do airline miles expire?",
    a: "It depends on the program. Some expire after a stretch of account inactivity, some carry a hard expiry date regardless of activity, and a few don't expire at all. Because a single qualifying transaction often resets the clock, an account that looks dormant may be fine. Our guide lists the current rule for every program we cover, with the date it was checked.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where can I buy airline miles?",
    a: "From the airline directly, or from an independent broker like us. Buying direct is always available and is usually the most expensive route, because a program has little reason to undercut the cash fares it is also trying to sell. Whichever way you go, compare the per-mile price against the cash fare for the seat you actually want before you buy.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is a good price for buying airline miles?",
    a: rateRange
      ? `There's no single number, because every program prices its own miles and every broker prices differently again. Across the ${rates.length} programs where our rate is confirmed, it runs from ${rateRange} per mile, and each program page shows its own rate beside its order limits and delivery window. The figure that decides it is whether the miles come to less than the cash fare for your seat, which the calculator on each page works out.`
      : "There's no single number, because every program prices its own miles and every broker prices differently again. Each program page shows its rate beside its order limits and delivery window, and the calculator works out whether the miles come to less than the cash fare for the seat you want.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is it cheaper to buy miles than to buy the ticket?",
    a: "Sometimes, and it's worth checking rather than assuming. It's most often true for premium cabins, one-way fares and upgrades, where the cash price runs high relative to the mileage price. It's least often true for a cheap domestic economy seat. Every program page has a calculator that puts your real cash fare against the cost of buying the miles, and when cash wins it tells you to pay cash.",
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="FAQ | Buying Airline Miles, Answered | buyflightmiles"
  description={`Answers on buying airline miles: what it costs, how fast miles arrive, how payment works and whether it's worth it. ${airlines.length} programs covered.`}
  slug="faq"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[
        { label: "Home", href: "/" },
        { label: "FAQ" },
      ]} />
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
        <h1>Buying airline miles, answered</h1>
        <p>
          The questions people actually ask before a first order. If yours isn't
          here, <a href="/contact">ask us directly</a> — a real answer beats a guess.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="spec" style="margin-top:0">
        <span class="spec-it">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="12 2 2 7 12 12 22 7 12 2"/><polyline points="2 17 12 22 22 17"/><polyline points="2 12 12 17 22 12"/></svg>
          <b>{airlines.length}</b>&nbsp;programs
        </span>
        {cheapest && (
          <span class="spec-it">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.59 13.41l-7.17 7.17a2 2 0 0 1-2.83 0L2 12V2h10l8.59 8.59a2 2 0 0 1 0 2.83z"/><line x1="7" y1="7" x2="7.01" y2="7"/></svg>
            from <b>{cheapest.pricePerMile}¢</b>&nbsp;per mile
          </span>
        )}
        {fastest && (
          <span class="spec-it">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
            delivery from <b>{fastest.delivery.replace("Within ", "")}</b>
          </span>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wash-cool" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container">
      <Faq delivery={fastest ? fastest.delivery.replace("Within ", "") : "24 hours"} extras={extras} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Questions about a specific program</h2>
        <p>Each program page answers its own: the rate, the order limits, the delivery window and the quirks that change how you should buy and spend those miles.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="related related-inline">
        <span class="related-lab">Programs</span>
        {airlines.map((a) => (
          <a href={`/buy/${a.slug}`}>{a.airline}</a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wash-cool">
    <div class="container ctaband">
      <div>
        <h3>Still have a question?</h3>
        <p>Ask before you order — we'd rather answer it than have you guess. Free, no-obligation, and nothing is charged on this site.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact">Contact us</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
